WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.255
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:05.370
ROBERT KUBIS: Hi.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.240
I'm Robert Kubis.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.620
And I'd like to introduce
you to Google Cloud

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.825
Spanner, a fully managed
relational database service.

00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:15.090
Cloud Spanner functions just
like a traditional database,

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.250
except that it's
horizontal scalable,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.940
while still providing ACID
transactions and SQL semantics.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.370
And we don't give any
performance up for that.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.980
Now, this is important
because if you have ever

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.350
deployed a global
multi-regional database,

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.960
you know that there are
certain steps from configuring

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.750
machines, setting up
replication, and failover,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.250
and design your sharding.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.460
And keeping all of that running
can be a challenge, too.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.365
While you may not encounter
all these challenges today,

00:00:41.365 --> 00:00:43.740
knowing that you don't have
to worry about them while you

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.410
cross various scalability
boundaries in the future

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.290
will give you a great
deal of peace of mind.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.350
To give you a picture of just
how amazing Cloud Spanner is,

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:56.010
let me start by showing you
how easy it is to get started.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.130
With Cloud Spanner,
we turn all that chaos

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.540
that I just talked
about into one screen.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.700
You just name the instance,
choose single or multi-region--

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.010
we pick Europe West here--

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.050
and how many nodes you want.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.350
I pick 42, because that is
always the right answer.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.150
We click Create,
and in seconds we

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.292
have a distributed
database at our disposal.

00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:17.820
But let's talk about a
more practical example.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.130
Imagine you are a ticket
seller selling tickets

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.570
to events worldwide.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.450
It's pretty important that you
only sell each ticket once,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.910
that buyers experience a
high-performance website,

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.730
and that everyone sees
the same ticket inventory.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.620
For this example,
we have deployed

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.250
Cloud Spanner in three Google
regions, US, Europe, and Asia,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.500
all connected by our
really high-performant

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.180
private global network.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.920
We are running
workloads that emulate

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.670
customers buying billions of
tickets for one million events.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.000
Here's the schema
of our application.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.260
You see tables with columns
that can be joined together,

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.160
just like in any other
relational database.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.230
There are significant numbers of
rows for some of those tables,

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:59.010
so this is a really large
relational database.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.320
In our demo app, each
purchase transaction

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.200
involves a complex mix
of reads of multiple rows

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.450
from and writes to
many of these tables.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.509
We are checking the
availability of tickets,

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.540
fetching account information,
and update ticket owners

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:13.560
and calendars.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.590
We can even run SQL queries
against this database

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.790
while it's handling
all that load.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.490
In the Google Cloud
Developer Console,

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.400
you can find the query
entry under your database.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.710
In our case here, we are
looking at a query that

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.840
checks the availability of
tickets on May 12th at 7:00

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.240
PM in Germany, all without
blocking those critical buy

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.250
transactions that are
happening all around the world.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.230
What's important here
is what you don't see

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.110
and how unremarkable
this SQL looks like.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.900
There's no additional complexity
in the query from a sharding

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:43.590
system.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.170
There's no need to join
across multiple shards

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.380
of your database or
anything you like.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.390
With Spanner, you can just
simply undust your SQL skills

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.510
and put them to work again
on a massive, scalable,

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.060
horizontally-distributed
database.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.770
And to help you
optimize your queries,

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.320
you have the possibility
to inspect the query plan

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.180
before or after
running your query,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.250
right in the Developer Console.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.970
Now real world applications
and workloads change over time,

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.520
and your database needs to
adjust while your business

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.560
keeps running.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.420
Cloud Spanner lets you make
transactional consistent schema

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.520
updates with no downtime at all.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.680
You can simply add
or change columns

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.510
while your workload is running.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.430
And what happens
if your app grows

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.010
and you need to add capacity?

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.670
With traditional
systems, you have

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.710
to figure out how
to scale up, get

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.800
an even more powerful server,
and upgrade the hardware.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.810
Or you can try to
scale out, but that

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.310
involves lots of
complexity, middleware,

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.150
and adds operational
risk, and takes time.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.610
In Cloud Spanner, I go
to the Developer Console,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:45.050
edit my instance, add more
nodes, and click Save.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:45.900
That's it.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.690
That's the power of a
fully-managed, horizontally

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.730
scalable SQL database.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.040
And of course, since we
designed Spanner at Google

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.990
to scale with our
business, we made

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.750
sure it can handle
very large databases.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.930
Let's switch over to our
monitoring tool, Stackdriver.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:04.080
The graphs show the history
of our demo database.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.730
And there are a couple of
things to point out here.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:08.490
First, in the upper
right, you will

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.650
see that when we
set up the database,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.870
we were able to load data
at over 800 megabytes

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.300
per second sustainable.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.630
That even peaked at around
1 gigabyte per second.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.579
That's just crazy fast, right?

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:22.830
Second, you can see,
on the lower left,

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:25.440
we run the whole demo
against a database

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.320
that is over 80 terabyte
of logical data.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.220
Let's recap.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.650
You can create a highly
reliable, performant,

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.040
fully-managed, multi-regional
database in seconds.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.675
Cloud Spanner is a
relational database

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:39.690
with the power of SQL
and ACID transactions

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:43.290
plus advanced features like
versioning, strong consistency

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.360
and online schema changes.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.840
You can scale out and in
as needed, fully managed,

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.180
and you don't have to
worry about replication.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.840
Your system even automatically
tunes itself for performance.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.290
And last, but not least, Cloud
Spanner is built for scale.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.530
Google has been running the
largest operational dataset

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.630
on it globally for years.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.200
Check out Cloud Spanner today
and find more information,

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.900
like best practices and
case studies of Spanner,

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.720
on cloud.google.com/spanner.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

